      "writtenOn": "2022-05-03T09:59:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks Paul. I assume the questions are aimed at Liz, not me. I\u0027m also interested in the answers.\n\nI had the impression that we need the versioned snapshots and `uses_sdks` to be able to evolve the Mainline module APIs independently of the platform SDK levels (cf. b/186201286). How will that work?",
      "parentUuid": "0a92b546_8bd906eb",
      "revId": "7eed538208b6b158daa39504122545450db6c2e3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dbe04ec7_7586e87c",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1066075
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-05-04T07:24:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Isn\u0027t the fact that code is unused enough reason to remove it? If it\u0027s unused it has a maintenance cost (and Soong is difficult enough to understand already) but no benefit and it looks like this code was in that category.\n\nb/186201286#comment5 and later seems to indicate that you don\u0027t agree whether versioned snapshots are necessary, either? I certainly don\u0027t know enough of your plans to tell and neither does anyone on the Android build team, which is not great because we are responsible for Soong and therefore should understand what it does...",
      "parentUuid": "422e8bc7_dfee25a5",
      "revId": "7eed538208b6b158daa39504122545450db6c2e3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4446a4f1_6163732b",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1039338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-05-04T09:47:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Isn\u0027t the fact that code is unused enough reason to remove it?\n\nNot necessarily. It depends on whether it is going to stay unused.\n\n\u003e b/186201286#comment5 and later seems to indicate that you don\u0027t agree whether versioned snapshots are necessary, either?\n\nFTR While I did make sure that the tests/build were not broken by any changes I made in that area I did not write it. So, yes it did have a maintenance cost and I was the one that paid most of it.\n\nI don\u0027t know whether it would have been needed. It is possible that it would have been useful for solving some future issues which we have not yet encountered because we have not yet done any serious development against prebuilts. That is what it was created in the first place. I did try to see if it was usable but found a number of problems and gave up.\n\n\u003e I certainly don\u0027t know enough of your plans to tell and neither does anyone on the Android build team, which is not great because we are responsible for Soong and therefore should understand what it does...\n\nCommunication goes both ways.\n\nGiven that the code has been in there for a long time and someone may have been relying on it, it seems to me that the responsibility is on the person who wants to remove it to at a minimum post on soong-discuss@ to check whether anyone has plans for it.\n\nIf they had I would have told them that removing it is only one small part of the cleanup. There is also:\n1. The logic in the sdk snapshot generation for generating a versioned snapshot.\n2. All the existing versioned snapshots in the different repositories that need cleaning up too. Although having snapshots of the released SDKs will be helpful for historical reasons so this may just involve renaming the Android.bp so it is no longer processed by Soong.\n3. The build scripts that have to handle different versions of the snapshot.\n4. The SDK finalization process when a new set of APIs is released.\n\nI think all that work justifies having a bug for it. Is there a specific bug for this work. If not there should be.\n\nWho is going to do that work?",
      "parentUuid": "dbe04ec7_7586e87c",
      "revId": "7eed538208b6b158daa39504122545450db6c2e3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5f120f1a_01040785",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1373864
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-05-04T10:37:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t mind all this complexity going away, but first I\u0027d like to know that there\u0027s a plan to support versioning of SDKs between modules. All this was done to cater for that, and it\u0027s based on Jiyong\u0027s design in go/android-module-sdk. A lot of water has flowed under the bridge since then though, and I don\u0027t really know what the intended approach is these days. E.g. are we going to limit ourselves to platform SDK levels between modules as well? @hansson probably knows this best.\n\nThe reason it\u0027s not in use is that, to my knowledge, no one has had any need to evolve APIs between modules yet, at least not when the SDKs for them need to be prebuilt. In practice that means the ART module dependencies since ART is the only module that has adopted the thin manifest with prebuilt SDKs approach.\n\nIt\u0027s not fully implemented either, as Paul points out. If it was I\u0027d probably have started using it for the ART module. Instead we currently work around this problem with a combination of avoiding to evolve those APIs, keep using old prebuilts, and relying on NDK versioning instead. We\u0027re a new module since SC so that has been relatively painless so far, but I don\u0027t expect this ad-hoc approach to work for very long.\n\nThen again, we should perhaps rethink the approach with prebuilt SDKs altogether? I don\u0027t think it\u0027d be sustainable to keep all this scaffolding in place if ART is the only module using it (originally the intention was that all modules would move to this model eventually). Maybe multi-tree will give us a better way to deal with it?",
      "parentUuid": "4446a4f1_6163732b",
      "revId": "7eed538208b6b158daa39504122545450db6c2e3",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
